WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.840
People really do not want the state

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.040
putting its fingers in their lives.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:07.920
Coming up

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.960
Reason TV travels to Brazil

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.800
for a look at how a group of libertarian activists

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.460
helped bring down a president.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.840
It definitely says great things

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.680
about Brazilian
democracy.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.360
Why is libertarianism catching on so fast in Brazil?

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.780
And that is how you win over undecided voters right there.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.480
More than understand our ideas

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.040
people have
to like our ideas.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.540
If they want to be cool they don't need to
be on the left.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.980
They can be in our group.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.140
Now this is kind of my life.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.680
Can these young activists help defeat the
big government populism,

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.860
stifling the economy

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.920
of Latin America’s most populous nation?

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.220
This is the worst recession we've had

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.620
in recorded history.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.500
It's like an office wannabe,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.720
but we are not
like serious guys,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.520
so it's not technically an office.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.120
In this cavernous production studio,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.260
a group of mostly twenty-somethings work through the night,

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.280
often sleeping by their desks.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.040
They’re making videos, uploading content
to social media,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.019
and strategizing.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:22.780
We're a group of friends,

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.220
and things do not work well

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.660
if the guys who enter into our team

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:28.880
don't become a friend of ours.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.560
This is the headquarters of the Free Brazil Movement.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.020
It’s the centerpiece of the country's growing libertarian movement,

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:39.680
And the group has already helped bring about a remarkable turn of events.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.800
The Free Brazil Movement has been a driving force

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.940
behind the ongoing impeachment

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.540
of Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff,

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.580
who in May was forced to step down.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.960
A left-wing populist,

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.760
Rousseff was impeached for fiscal impropriety.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.400
It definitely says great things about Brazilian democracy.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.600
We always had politicians just running free

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.620
and doing whatever they wanted with public money.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.920
So this is a very, very big deal.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.160
But the impeachment effort probably would have fizzled

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.920
if the Free Brazil Movement  hadn't helped organize

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.840
a succession of demonstrations

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.920
that involved millions of anti-Rousseff protesters.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.100
The anger of the Brazilian people with their
government

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.000
was cast in explicitly libertarian terms.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:36.540
In Brazil,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.560
all the people are libertarian

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.740
in many ways,

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:41.580
like, people really do not want

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.160
the state putting its fingers in their lives.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.960
When you start to explain those things

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.100
people, 'oh yeah, the government's way too big

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.740
yeah, yeah, this is the real problem.'

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.120
To keep the pressure on for removing Rousseff from office,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.800
members of the Free Brazil Movement

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.520
led a 33-day march from Sao Paulo

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.580
to the federal capital of Brasilia.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.000
It was a 750-mile trek,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.680
which they did while carrying an impeachment bill to deliver to congress.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.340
After arriving in Brasilia,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.220
they sat down with congressional leaders

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.480
to make their case.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.300
We are going to defeat the socialists in Brazil

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.980
the way that they hate most:

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.360
by democracy.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.960
Brazil is now in the second year of recession

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.840
and this is the worst recession we've had

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.320
in recorded history.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.880
The Brazilian economy has been contracting
over the last couple years,

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:59.920
and the unemployment rate recently climbed

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.080
above 11 percent.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.840
On the 2016 Index of Economic Freedom,

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.080
which categorizes countries

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.810
based on the level government interference in the private economy,

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:12.160
Brazil ranked 122.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.400
Starting a new business in Brazil

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.220
takes about 14 times as long as it does in the United States,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.500
according to the World Bank.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.160
Here’s the big mystery in Brazil

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.280
and in much of Latin America:

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.460
How is it that so many

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.740
citizens continue to believe that government
can solve their countries’ problems

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.140
despite so much lived experience to the contrary?

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.780
The theory behind the libertarian movement in Brazil

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.940
is that most people derive their politics not from reasoning

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.020
but from culture.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:49.020
So the way to change public policy is to create
a new cultural identity that’s all about

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:49.620
freedom.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.800
If you want to be cool, you have to be from
the left.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.640
If you are not from the left,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.160
you are a guy who don't like gays,

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.940
you don't like

00:04:58.940 --> 00:04:59.660
poor people,

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.460
you don't like black people.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.400
What is the [Free] Brazil Movement?

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.860
Brazil Movement is other way
that young guy or a young girl

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:08.360
go.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.160
They don't need to be on the left group now.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.560
They can be in our group.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.700
To promote this new libertarian cultural identity,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.940
the Free Brazil Movement functions in part

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.700
as an alternative media outlet

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.020
with its more than 1.3 million Facebook followers.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.920
The media in Brazil receives lots of money
from the government,

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.440
and we had to create

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.480
a sort of parallel media.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.920
In terms of Facebook, we're stronger than any politician in Brazil, and

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.360
we have more views,

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.480
more reach,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.620
than any, any media group.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:52.860
As a video production shop,

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.540
the Free Brazil
Movement scored one of its biggest hits

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.420
in 2014

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.620
with a joke advertisement for Paulo Batista,

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.940
a long-shot candidate for the Sao Paulo state legislature.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.720
This is a politician flying

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:07.880
with a tie

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.920
and spreading the libertarian message

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.520
using a beam that comes from his eyes

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.180
that turns bad public services

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:18.180
into good private services.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.680
It drew a front page story in The New York
Times

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.520
and was featured on the John Oliver show.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.720
Perhaps my favorite candidate this year is
this guy—

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.220
Paulo Batista,

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:30.300
who seems to be running

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.160
on a platform of having laser vision

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.200
that can transform the country

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.220
and who ends the ad by flying through space for twenty solid seconds.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:41.740
We have not edited this.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.780
This is genuinely how his campaign ad ends.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.640
And that is how you win over undecided voters right there.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.160
The video was aimed not at promoting Batista’s candidacy

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.100
but at rebranding the concept of
privatization.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.220
Privatizing that was a word that the left
made a disaster

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.100
in terms of public policy.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.480
It became something that people, 'oh it's nice',

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.620
because people were starting to use as a meme.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:09.200
Like,

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.160
'oh, I had a girlfriend who is not looking pretty nice in this picture,'

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.740
so she receives the privatizing beam

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:17.340
and she becomes hot.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.220
We have footage from our demonstrations

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.460
where like thousands of people are screaming in the middle of avenues,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.260
'we should privatize those public companies now!'

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:36.260
Critics of the Free Brazil Movement say that its message has mostly caught on

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:39.060
with the middle class in Sao Paulo and the South,

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:42.560
making less traction in Brazil’s far poorer Northern states.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.300
Brazil is a very, very poor country,

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.500
so to be fiscally conservative

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:49.580
you have to convince,

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.100
and this is the hard part,

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:54.080
you have to convince
people that being fiscally conservative

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.140
means

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.770
in the medium to longer term,

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.770
having more
money to spend in education and health.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:19.160
Enter 20-year-old Kim Kataguiri,

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.200
the grandson of Japanese immigrants,

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.780
a part-time law student in Sao Paulo,

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:26.580
and the Free Brazil Movement's
biggest star.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:30.260
One of his specialties is communicating how free market reforms

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.200
benefit the poor.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.480
Just observe what the left has already done.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:36.520
They've created the perfect formula.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:37.920
More than understand our ideas,

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.420
people have to like our ideas,

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:41.260
so i think that's the great secret.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:42.020
 

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:45.360
We have like 205 million people in Brazil.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.840
I mean you will never make 205 million people understand

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.940
any idea in a profound way.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.320
Education, for an example.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.240
We don't explain that the voucher system is more efficient

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:58.100
and it has worked in other countries,

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:01.860
and the private companies can take better care of the schools.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:02.640
We just ask,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.020
'So do you want the poor people
to study in the same school as the rich people?'

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:10.240
I mean it really looks like
a leftist speech,

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:11.840
but we're defending the voucher system.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:19.820
 

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.240
Selling to the people ideals of not only liberty,

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.860
but also of equality.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:25.940
And showing that equality is not only a value of the leftists,

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:27.880
but we also can do it.

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:36.380
On a Thursday evening, a group of college
students meet up at at a bar

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.880
in a residential neighborhood of Sao Paulo.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.660
They’re members of Students for Liberty,

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:45.240
an international organization that brings together libertarians on campus.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:47.500
It has a presence in 107 countries

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:48.720
including the U.S.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.240
But based on the count of local coordinators who've signed up with the group,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.860
the Brazilian chapter is bigger than Students for Liberty in every other country combined.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.140
Everybody can see that government is not working for us as it should.

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:08.240
I was thinking if there is an option, and I
discovered that there is.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.300
And now this is kind of my life.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:34.860
The reason Students for Liberty’s has seen
exponential growth in Brazil

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:36.600
in contrast to other countries

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.360
is something of a mystery.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.540
Mariana Lion points to the collapse of socialism in the region

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.040
—particularly in Venezuela.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.140
So I think that this is the secret,

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:48.520
is when everybody starts to realize

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.040
that we can go from here

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.140
to somewhere worse.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.180
It may also be that the Brazilian branch of Students for Liberty

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:58.940
has particularly good organizational tactics.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:02.480
Coordinator Roni Stefanuto Rodrigues recruits new members

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:04.680
by spending about two hours a day

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:05.680
listening in

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:08.800
as strangers discuss the
latest news on Facebook.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:12.280
When he finds someone making libertarian-sounding
arguments,

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:12.900
he reaches out.

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:15.120
It's like a headhunter.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.890
They are on the way to becoming a libertarian,

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:20.200
and I always say, 'hey, I have this book,

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:21.160
do you want to read?'

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.720
The libertarian movement in Brazil would never have gotten off the ground

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:37.840
if it weren’t for a handful of think tanks

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:41.060
that started laying the groundwork many years ago.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:43.620
One major player is Mises Brasil,

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:48.160
which occupies a shared office in this building in Downtown Sao Paulo,

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:52.440
and is unaffiliated with the U.S.-based
think tank with the same name.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.840
The chief of operations is 24-year-old Geanluca Lorenzon.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:57.860
The question that I ask myself was,

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.760
'Why do we talk about freedom in our songs and our lyrics

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.380
and in our poetry all the time,

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:04.380
but simply this
doesn't reflect in politics.'

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.740
And the reason is because we didn't have content.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:11.320
Mises Brasil publishes daily articles on libertarian issues

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.240
and gets about a million page views per month.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.040
It's also both commissioned and brought back into print

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.680
Portuguese translations of the major works of Austrian economics.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:24.420
Mises Brazil was founded in 2007

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.940
by investment banker Helio Beltrao.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:31.360
He says the intellectual climate in Brazil
has undergone a profound shift.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.940
They would look at me like, 'Helio has read something that made him crazy.'

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:37.180
And I think that changed,

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:38.500
over the last ten years,

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:39.740
or maybe less,

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:41.780
and now they only think I'm radical.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:50.160
Now that libertarian activists have helped push Dilma Rousseff out of office,

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.240
where does that leave the country?

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.360
Rousseff was replaced by her vice president,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:55.700
Michel Temer.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:59.760
A former congressman who has been in politics for nearly thirty-years,

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:03.320
Temer has been implicated in Brazil’s ongoing corruption scandals.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:05.160
On a scale from one to ten,

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.160
ten being the
most corrupt,

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:08.840
I would say he's a six.

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:12.940
Though libertarians have little faith in Temer the person,

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:15.760
they say the impeachment has created political incentives

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:18.160
that are leading him
to make some positive changes.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:21.800
What Temer is betting his government on is

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.600
to bring about much needed economic reforms

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.120
to get unemployment down.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.180
And he's counting on this to happen

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:31.440
in like

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:32.720
six or eight months

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.240
so he can stay in office.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:37.660
The politicians have interest in maximize
their time in office,

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:40.520
and if we turn libertarian ideas

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:41.920
politically profitable,

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:43.920
then either way we're gonna win.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:46.140
And I think it's good not to have this populist figure

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.800
of the person who brought us freedom.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:51.120
Who will bring freedom to the people

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:52.820
is the ideas themselves.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.600
The impeachment is just the start of the game,

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:58.040
and we should put the pressure over him,

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:00.060
kick all of those corrupt guys, who are there,

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:01.880
and they are still there,

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.780
and pressure him to make the good economical

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:06.920
and the good democratic reforms that we need

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:08.600
to change our political system.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:12.740
I don't see any way of changing policy

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:15.260
besides getting involved in politics.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:19.280
There’s also a movement afoot to elect actual libertarians

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:20.880
to political office.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:23.760
Thirty-one-year-old economist Fabio Ostermann

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:26.040
has been an important behind-the-scenes figure

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:27.720
in libertarian circles.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:30.240
And now he’s running
for mayor of Puerto Alegre,

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.360
a southern city with a population of about 1.5 million.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:35.440
I think Brazilian libertarians

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.680
are getting ready to take this next step.

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.560
Ostermann has led a takeover of Brazil's

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.000
Social Liberal Party,

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:43.320
which is planning to put forward

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.640
many more libertarian political candidates

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:47.280
in the near future.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.160
We're a party that defends both gay marriage,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.120
decriminalization of marijuana,

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:54.120
and also of course free markets,

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:55.360
free trade,

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:56.880
deregulation of the economy,

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.000
privatizations.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.600
And we know that we will be displeasing a lot of people

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.360
by defending these ideas.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.360
But we feel like it's right thing to do.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:08.680
We're not yet a libertarian country.

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:11.360
We're actually very far from that, unfortunately.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:15.160
But this is a very special moment for libertarians in Brazil

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:18.200
because we're showing up as an option,

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:20.640
as an alternative.

